### Configure Regex Naming Pattern  
#### Configure dynamic indexing for fields with naming patterns using regex.  
To index fields matching a naming pattern, use a [View](/docs/search/about/view-support#std-label-fts-transform-documents-collections) to transform your data so that the fields to index are nested in a sub-document. This enables you to use dynamic mappings for the sub-document path so that you can automatically search all new fields with the suffix `_type`, without any changes to your index definition.  
The following `View` named `listings_SearchableTypes` only matches field names that end with `_type` in the `sample_airbnb.listingsAndReviews` collection. Specifically, the [`$set`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/set/#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-set) stage adds a new field named `searchable_types`, which contains the filtered fields with the term `_type` in the field name. The [`$arrayToObject`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/arrayToObject/#mongodb-expression-exp.-arrayToObject) contains the filter input (entire document) and condition (regex match for `_type`).  
1. Connect to your cluster using [`mongosh`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/mongodb-shell/#mongodb-binary-bin.mongosh).  
To learn more, see [Connect to a Cluster via mongosh.](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/mongo-shell-connection/#std-label-connect-mongo-shell)  
2. Switch to the `sample_airbnb` database.  
```sh
use sample_airbnb
```  
3. Create a View named `listings_SearchableTypes`.  
```sh
db.createView(
"listings_SearchableTypes",
"listingsAndReviews",
[
{
"$set": {
"searchable_types": {
"$arrayToObject": {
"$filter": {
"input": { "$objectToArray": "$$ROOT" },
"cond": {
"$regexMatch": {
"input": "$$this.k",
"regex": /_type$/
}
}
}
}
}
}
}
]
)
```  
