## Define the Index for the `boolean` Type  
1. Retrieve your Service Account access token for the project.  
Use your service account to generate an access token, which authenticates your requests to the Atlas Administration API. To learn how to generate an access token, see [Make an API (Application Programming Interface) Request.](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/configure-api-access/#std-label-example-api-request)  
2. Make an API (Application Programming Interface) call.  
Replace the following values in the `curl` command below. This command sends a `POST` request to your cluster to create a MongoDB Search index with the `boolean` field type.  
- Replace \{`ACCESS-TOKEN`} with the output from the preceding step  
- Replace \{`groupId`} with the project ID of the project where you want to create the MongoDB Search index  
- Replace \{`clusterName`} with the name of the cluster where you want to create the MongoDB Search index  
```shell
curl --header "Authorization: Bearer ${ACCESS_TOKEN}" \
--header "Accept: application/vnd.atlas.2025-03-12+json" \
--header "Content-Type: application/json" \
-X POST "https://cloud.mongodb.com/api/atlas/v2/groups/{groupId}/clusters/{clusterName}/search/indexes" \
-d '{ "collectionName": "<collectionName>",
"database": "<databaseName>",
"name": "default",
"type": "search",
"definition": {
"mappings": {
"dynamic": true | false,
"fields": {
"<fieldName>": {
"type": "boolean",
}
}
}
}
}'
```

## Define the Index for the `boolean` Type  
1. Create a `search-index.json` configuration file and specify the `boolean` type.  
```json
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": true | false,
"fields": {
"<fieldName>": {
"type": "boolean"
}
}
}
}
```  
2. Connect to the Atlas CLI  
In your terminal, connect to your local deployment from the Atlas CLI. To learn more, see [Connect from the Atlas CLI.](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/cli/current/connect-atlas-cli/)  
3. Create a MongoDB Search index.  
Run the [atlas clusters search indexes create](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/cli/current/command/atlas-clusters-search-indexes-create/) command in your terminal, replacing `<path-to-file>` with the path to the `search-index.json` file:  
```shell
atlas clusters search indexes create --clusterName <cluster-name> --file <path-to-file>
```

## Try an Example for the `boolean` Type  
The following index definition examples use collections in the [sample data](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/sample-data/#std-label-available-sample-datasets). After you [load the sample data](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/import/load-sample-data/#std-label-load-sample-data), you can use these examples to index fields as the `boolean` type.  
To learn how to set up and run the following example code, see [Define the Index for the token Type](/docs/search/index/field-types/token-type#std-label-define-index-token-admin-api).  
The following example creates a MongoDB Search index on the `sample_analytics.customers` collection and indexes the `active` field as the `boolean` type:  
```json
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": false,
"fields": {
"active": {
"type": "boolean"
}
}
}
}
```  
You can use the MongoDB Search `boolean` type to index `true` and `false` values. You can query fields of type `boolean` using the [equals](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/equals#std-label-equals-ref) and [in](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/in#std-label-in-ref) operators. You can also [sort](/docs/search/query/sort#std-label-sort-ref) by indexed `boolean` fields.  
You can also use the `boolean` type to index:  
- Fields whose value is an array of booleans. To learn more, see [How to Index the Elements of an Array.](/docs/search/index/field-types/array-type#std-label-fts-array-ref)  
- Boolean fields inside an array of documents indexed as the [embeddedDocuments](/docs/search/index/field-types/embedded-documents-type#std-label-bson-data-types-embedded-documents) type.  
To facet on `boolean` values, use [MongoDB View](/docs/search/about/view-support#std-label-fts-transform-documents-collections) to convert the value to `string` type. For a demonstration of transforming an `boolean` type to `string` type for faceting, see [Transform Fields for Faceting.](/docs/search/about/view-support#std-label-fts-transform-for-facet)  
If you enable [dynamic mappings](/docs/search/index/define-field-mappings#std-label-static-dynamic-mappings), MongoDB Search automatically indexes fields of type `boolean`. You can use the **Visual Editor** or the  **JSON Editor** in the Atlas UI to index fields as the `boolean` type.

