## Syntax  
The `sort` option has the following syntax:  
```json
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": true|false,
"fields": {
"<field-name>": {
"type": "boolean" | "date" | "number" | "objectId" | "token" | "uuid"
}
}
},
"sort": { "<field-to-sort>": 1 | -1 } | { "<field-to-sort>": { "order": 1 | -1, "noData": "lowest" | "highest" } }
}
```

### Simple Sort Order  
The following index definition sorts the `number_of_reviews` and the `last_reviewed` fields in descending order.  
```json
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": false,
"fields": {
"number_of_reviews": {
"type": "number"
},
"last_reviewed": {
"type": "date"
}
}
},
"sort": { "number_of_reviews": -1, "last_reviewed": -1 }
}
```

### Sort With Null and Missing Values  
The following index definition sorts the `number_of_reviews`  and the `last_reviewed` fields in descending order. It places documents with null or missing values at the bottom of the results.  
```json
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": false,
"fields": {
"number_of_reviews": {
"type": "number"
},
"last_reviewed": {
"type": "date"
}
}
},
"sort": {
"number_of_reviews": { "order": -1, "noData": "lowest" },
"last_reviewed": { "order": -1, "noData": "lowest" }
}
}
```

### Sort on Child Embedded Document Fields  
The following index definition sorts on the `date` field in descending order. To sort on `date` field, it defines the `reviews` field as the `embeddedDocuments` type and the `document` type.  
```json
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": false,
"fields": {
"reviews": [
{
"type": "embeddedDocuments",
"dynamic": true
},
{
"type": "document",
"dynamic": false,
"fields": {
"date": {
"type": "date"
}
}
}
]
}
},
"sort": { "reviews.date": -1 }
}
```

### Sort on Mixed Field Types  
The following index definition sorts on the `property_type` field in ascending order with null and missing values appearing at the bottom of the results. The `property_type` field is defined as the `string`, `autocomplete`, and `token` types. The index uses only the `token` type for sorting.  
```json
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": false,
"fields": {
"property_type": [
{
"type": "string"
},
{
"type": "autocomplete",
"tokenization": "edgeGram",
"minGrams": 2,
"maxGrams": 15
},
{
"type": "token"
}
]
}
},
"sort": { "property_type": { "order": 1, "noData": "highest" } }
}
```

## Syntax  
The `storedSource` option has the following syntax in an [index definition:](/docs/search/index/index-definitions#std-label-ref-index-definitions)  
```json
{
"storedSource": true | false | {
"include" | "exclude": [
"<field-name>",
...
]
}
}
```

## Examples  
The following index examples use the fields in the `sample_mflix.movies` collection to demonstrate how to configure the fields to store on MongoDB Search using the `storedSource` option. You can use [`mongosh`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/mongodb-shell/#mongodb-binary-bin.mongosh), the Atlas UI Visual Editor, or the Atlas UI JSON Editor to configure the indexes.  
The following example stores only the `title` and `awards.wins` fields in the documents in the collection. After you select your preferred configuration method, select the `movies` collection under the `sample_mflix` database.  
Click Refine Your Index to configure your index.  
In the Stored Source Fields section, click Specified.  
Select `awards.wins` from the dropdown in the Field Name column and click Add.  
Click Add Field to specify another field to store.  
Select `title` from the dropdown in the Field Name column and click Add.  
Click Save Changes.  
The following example stores only the `title` and `awards.wins` fields in the documents in the collection. After you select your preferred configuration method, select the `movies` collection under the `sample_mflix` database.  
Append lines 5 to 10 from the following example to the index definition to store the specified fields on MongoDB Search. The example uses the [dot notation](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/core/document/#std-label-document-dot-notation) to specify nested fields.  
```json
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": true,
},
"storedSource": {
"include": [
"title",
"awards.wins"
]
}
}
```  
The following example stores all fields except `directors` and `imdb.rating` in the documents in the collection. After you select your preferred configuration method, select the `movies` collection under the `sample_mflix` database.  
