## Syntax  
`compound` has the following syntax:  
```javascript
{
$search: {
"index": <index name>, // optional, defaults to "default"
"compound": {
<must | mustNot | should | filter>: [ { <clauses> } ],
"score": <options>,
"doesNotAffect": "<facet-to-exclude>" | [<array-of-facets>]
}
}
}
```  
Each `must`, `mustNot`, `should`, and `filter` clause contains an array of subclauses. Use array syntax even if the array contains only one subclause. See the [examples](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/compound#std-label-compound-examples) on this page.

## Options  
`compound` uses the following terms to construct a query:  
|  | Clauses that must match to for a document to be included in the results. The returned score is the sum of the scores of all the subqueries in the clause. Maps to the `AND` boolean operator. |
| --- | --- |
|  | Clauses that must not match for a document to be included in the results. `mustNot` clauses don't contribute to a returned document's score. Maps to the `AND NOT` boolean operator. |
|  | Clauses that you prefer to match in documents that are included in the results. Documents that contain a match for a `should` clause have higher scores than documents that don't contain a `should` clause. The returned score is the sum of the scores of all the subqueries in the clause. If you use more than one `should` clause, you can use the `minimumShouldMatch` option to specify a minimum number of `should` clauses that must match to include a document in the results. The value of the `minimumShouldMatch` must be less than or equal to the number of `should` clauses in the query. If omitted, the `minimumShouldMatch` option defaults to `0`. See an [example.](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/compound#std-label-min-example) Maps to the `OR` boolean operator. If you use only the `should` clause inside a compound query, the `compound` operator treats the array of the `should` clause queries as a logical `OR`. MongoDB Search must find a match for at least one `should` criteria to return any results. When you specify multiple `should` clause criteria with the `minimumShouldMatch` option set to `0`, MongoDB Search treats `minimumShouldMatch` as set to `1` and must match at least one criteria to return any results. |
|  | Clauses that must all match for a document to be included in the results. `filter` clauses do not contribute to a returned document's score. For example, you can replace the [`$match`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/match/#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-match) stage with the [`$search`](/docs/search/query/aggregation-stages/search#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-search) stage using the `compound` operator `filter` option. The following [`$match`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/match/#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-match) stage filters for documents with a given value for the field `role`: `$match: {
"role": { "$in": [ "CLIENT", "PROFESSIONAL" ] }
}` You can use the `compound` operator `filter` option instead: `$search: {
"compound": {
"filter": [{
"queryString": {
"defaultPath": "role",
"query": "CLIENT OR PROFESSIONAL"
}
}]
}
}` See another [filter example.](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/compound#std-label-filter-example) |
| `score` | Modify the [score](/docs/search/query/score/overview#std-label-scoring-ref) of the entire `compound` clause. You can use `score` to boost, replace, or otherwise alter the score. If you don't specify `score`, the returned score is the sum of the scores of all the subqueries in the `must` and `should` clauses that generated a match. To learn more, see [Scoring Behavior.](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/compound#std-label-compound-score) |
| `doesNotAffect` | Facet or list of facets to exclude from count recalculation on the basis of this query. Value must be one or more names of a facet defined in `$search.facets` or `$searchMeta.facets`. The operator uses the specified facets for filtering documents to match this query, but doesn't recalculate counts for them. For more information, see [Multi-Select Facets.](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/facet#std-label-fts-facet-multi-select) |

## Usage  
You can use any of the clauses with any top-level operator, such as [autocomplete](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/autocomplete#std-label-autocomplete-ref), [text](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/text#std-label-text-ref), or [span](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/span#std-label-span-ref), to specify query criteria.  
To optimize performance, place non-scoring operators such as [equals](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/equals#std-label-equals-ref), [range](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/range#std-label-range-ref), and [in](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/in#std-label-in-ref) in the [`filter`](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/compound#mongodb-data-filter) clause to avoid unnecessary scoring operations. If you want to run multiple operations that must be evaluated as an `OR` condition, nest a [`should`](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/compound#mongodb-data-should) clause within the top-level [`filter`.](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/compound#mongodb-data-filter)  
**Example:**  
```javascript
[
{
$search: {
index: "default",
compound: {
filter: [
{
compound: {
should: [
{
equals: {
path: "accommodates",
value: 2
}
},
{
range: {
path: "pricePerNight",
lte: 200
}
}
]
}
}
]
}
}
}
]
```

### Sample Queries  
The following queries demonstrate the [`$search`](/docs/search/query/aggregation-stages/search#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-search) `compound` operator in MongoDB Search queries.  
#### `must` and `mustNot` Example  
The following example uses a combination of `must` and `mustNot` clauses to contruct a query. The `must` clause uses the [text](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/text#std-label-text-ref) operator to search for the term `varieties` in the `description` field. For a document to match, it must fulfill the `must` clause. The `mustNot` clause performs a search operation for the term `apples` in the `description` field. For a document to match, it must *not* fulfill the `mustNot` clause.  
```javascript
db.fruit.aggregate([
{
"$search": {
"compound": {
"must": [{
"text": {
"query": "varieties",
"path": "description"
}
}],
"mustNot": [{
"text": {
"query": "apples",
"path": "description"
}
}]
}
}
}
])
```  
The above query returns the document with `_id: 3` because its `description` field contains the word `varieties` and does not contain `apples`.  
➤ Try this in the [MongoDB Search Playground.](https://search-playground.mongodb.com/tools/code-sandbox/snapshots/6613fd6e53a0260867247809)  
#### `must` and `should` Example  
The following queries use `must` to specify search conditions that must be met and `should` to specify preference for documents that contain the word `Fuji`.

### Basic Example  
For this query, the `$project` pipeline stage excludes all document fields except `_id` and adds a `score` field, which displays the document's relevance score.  
```javascript
db.fruit.aggregate([
{
"$search": {
"compound": {
"must": [{
"text": {
"query": "varieties",
"path": "description"
}
}],
"should": [{
"text": {
"query": "Fuji",
"path": "description"
}
}]
}
}
},
{
"$project": {
"score": { "$meta": "searchScore" }
}
}
])
```  
**Output:**  
```javascript
{ "_id" : 1, "score" : 0.6425117254257202 }
{ "_id" : 3, "score" : 0.21649497747421265 }
```  
The document with `_id: 1` has a higher score because its `description` field contains the word `Fuji`, satisfying the `should` clause.  
➤ Try this in the [MongoDB Search Playground.](https://search-playground.mongodb.com/tools/code-sandbox/snapshots/6613fe1753a026086724780b)  
#### minimumShouldMatch Example  
In a query with multiple `should` clauses, you can use the `miniumumShouldMatch` option to specify a minimum number of clauses which must match to return a result.  
The following query has one `must` clause and two `should` clauses, with a `minimumShouldMatch` value of `1`. A document must include the term `varieties` in the `description` field and must include either `Fuji` or `Golden Delicious` in the description field to be included in the result set.  
```javascript
db.fruit.aggregate([
{
$search: {
"compound": {
"must": [{
"text": {
"query": "varieties",
"path": "description"
}
}],
"should": [{
"text": {
"query": "Fuji",
"path": "description"
}
},
{
"text": {
"query": "Golden Delicious",
"path": "description"
}
}],
"minimumShouldMatch": 1
}
}
}
])
```  
**Output:**  
```javascript
{
"_id" : 1,
"type" : "apple",
"description" : "Apples come in several varieties, including Fuji, Granny Smith, and Honeycrisp.",
"category" : "nonorganic",
"in_stock" : false
}
```  
The document with `_id: 1` matches the `must` clause and the first of the two `should` clauses.  
➤ Try this in the [MongoDB Search Playground.](https://search-playground.mongodb.com/tools/code-sandbox/snapshots/6613ff6fbfc02dcf24ce9588)  
#### `filter` Examples  
`filter` behaves the same as `must`, except that the `filter` clause is not considered in a returned document's score, and therefore does not affect the order of the returned documents.  
### Basic Example  
The following query uses the following clauses:  
- `must` and `filter` to specify search conditions which must be met.  
- `should` to specify preference for documents containing the word  `banana`. The `should` clause doesn't include the `minimumShouldMatch` option. When you omit `minimumShouldMatch`, it defaults to `0`.  
```javascript
db.fruit.aggregate([
{
"$search": {
"compound": {
"must": [{
"text": {
"query": "varieties",
"path": "description"
}
}],
"should": [{
"text": {
"query": "banana",
"path": "description"
}
}],
"filter": [{
"text": {
"query": "granny",
"path": "description"
}
}]
}
}
}
])
```  
**Output:**  
```javascript
{
"_id" : 1,
"type" : "apple",
"description" : "Apples come in several varieties, including Fuji, Granny Smith, and Honeycrisp.",
"category" : "nonorganic",
"in_stock" : false

}
```  
The returned document fulfills all the requirements for inclusion:  
- Both the `must` clause and the `filter` clause match.  
- The `minimumShouldMatch` value is not specified, so it defaults to `0`. As a result, the `should` clause fails and still returns a document.  
➤ Try this in the [MongoDB Search Playground.](https://search-playground.mongodb.com/tools/code-sandbox/snapshots/6614008353a026086724780f)  
#### Nested Example  
The following example uses nested `compound` clauses to construct a query. For this example, the `fruit` collection has an index on the `type`, `category`, and `in_stock` fields, whose text fields use the [default](/docs/search/index/analyzers/standard#std-label-ref-standard-analyzer) analyzer. The query requires documents to only satisfy one of the following  `should` clauses:  
- Contain the word `apple` in the `type` field.  
- Contain the term `organic` in the `category` field and have the value `true` in the `in_stock` field.  
```javascript
db.fruit.aggregate([
{
$search: {
"compound": {
"should": [
{
"text": {
"query": "apple",
"path": "type"
}
},
{
"compound": {
"must": [
{
"text": {
"query": "organic",
"path": "category"
}
},
{
"equals": {
"value": true,
"path": "in_stock"
}
}
]
}
}
],
"minimumShouldMatch": 1
}
}
}
])
```  
**Output:**  
```javascript
{
"_id" : 3,
"type" : "pear",
"description" : "Bosc and Bartlett are the most common varieties of pears.",
"category" : "organic",
"in_stock" : true
}
{
"_id" : 1,
"type" : "apple",
"description" : "Apples come in several varieties, including Fuji, Granny Smith, and Honeycrisp.",
"category" : "nonorganic",
"in_stock" : false
}
```  
The documents in the results fulfill all the requirements for inclusion:  
- The document with `_id: 3` matches the `must` clause nested within the second `should` clause.  
- The document with `_id: 1` matches the first `should` clause.  
➤ Try this in the [MongoDB Search Playground.](https://search-playground.mongodb.com/tools/code-sandbox/snapshots/66140231bfc02dcf24ce958a)  
