## Migrate to the `vectorSearch` Operator  
The [`vectorSearch` (MongoDB Search Operator)](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/vectorSearch#std-label-fts-vectorSearch-ref) adds analyzed text capabilities to your semantic search. To use the [`vectorSearch` (MongoDB Search Operator)](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/vectorSearch#std-label-fts-vectorSearch-ref) in your [`$search`](/docs/search/query/aggregation-stages/search#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-search) queries against a vector field instead of `knnBeta`, complete the following steps:  
1. Ensure that your index definition uses the `vector` type.  
If your index definition uses the [knnVector](/docs/search/index/field-types/knn-vector#std-label-fts-data-types-knn-vector) type to index vector fields, replace `knnVector` in your index definition with the [vector](/docs/search/index/field-types/vector-type#std-label-bson-data-types-vector) type. To learn more, see [Migrate to `vector` Type.](/docs/search/index/field-types/knn-vector#std-label-migrate-knnVector-to-vector)  
2. Modify your queries to use the `vectorSearch` operator.  
The [`vectorSearch` (MongoDB Search Operator)](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/vectorSearch#std-label-fts-vectorSearch-ref) adds analyzed text capabilities alongside semantic search. To use the `vectorSearch` operator, modify your queries against `vector` type fields to use `vectorSearch` operator options.  
**Example:**  
Replace the query in the [Filter Example](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/knn-beta#std-label-knn-beta-egs) with the following:  
```javascript
db.embedded_movies.aggregate([
{
"$search": {
"index": "default",
"vectorSearch": {
"filter": {
"range": {
"lte": ISODate("1971-06-30 00:27:59.177000000"),
"path": "released"
}
},
"path": "plot_embedding",

"limit": 10,
"numCandidates": 1000
}
}
},
{
"$project": {
"_id": 0,
"title": 1,
"plot": 1,
"released": 1,
"score": { $meta: "searchScore" }
}
}
])
```  
For other examples that demonstrate advanced filtering options such fuzzy or phrase matching, see `vectorSearch` operator [Examples.](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/vectorSearch#std-label-fts-vectorSearch-egs)

## Syntax  
`moreLikeThis` has the following syntax:  
```javascript
{
"$search": {
"index": index name, // optional, defaults to "default"
"moreLikeThis": {
"like": [
{
<"field-name">: <"field-value">,
...
},
...
],
"score": <options>
}
}
}
```

### Example 1: Single Document with Multiple Fields  
The following example uses the `moreLikeThis` operator to find documents that are similar to multiple field values. For this example, the index defition contains [dynamic mappings](/docs/search/index/define-field-mappings#std-label-static-dynamic-mappings) to dynamically index all [dynamically indexable field types](/docs/search/index/define-field-mappings#std-label-bson-data-chart) in the collection. Your index definition for the `sample_mflix.movies` collection should look similar to the following.  
```text
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": true
}
}
```  
**Example:**  
The following query searches for movies that are similar to the input movie title "The Godfather" and input movie genre "action". It includes a [`$limit`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/limit/#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-limit) stage to limit the output to `5` results and a [`$project`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/project/#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-project) stage to exclude all fields except `title`, `released`, and `genres`.  
```javascript
db.movies.aggregate([
{
"$search": {
moreLikeThis: {
like:
{
"title": "The Godfather",
"genres": "action"
}
}
}
},
{ "$limit": 5},
{
$project: {
"_id": 0,
"title": 1,
"released": 1,
"genres": 1
}
}
])
```  
**Output:**  
```javascript
[
{ genres: [ 'Comedy', 'Drama', 'Romance' ], title: 'Godfather' },
{
genres: [ 'Crime', 'Drama' ],
title: 'The Godfather',
released: ISODate("1972-03-24T00:00:00.000Z")
},
{
genres: [ 'Crime', 'Drama' ],
title: 'The Godfather: Part II',
released: ISODate("1974-12-20T00:00:00.000Z")
},
{
genres: [ 'Crime', 'Drama' ],
title: 'The Godfather: Part III',
released: ISODate("1990-12-26T00:00:00.000Z")
},
{
genres: [ 'Action' ],
title: 'The Defender',
released: ISODate("1994-07-28T00:00:00.000Z")
}
]
```  
MongoDB Search results contain movies similar to the input movie title "The Godfather" and input movie genre "action".

### Example 2: Input Document Excluded in Results  
The following example uses `find()` to identify an input document, and then uses the `moreLikeThis` operator to find similar documents. For this example, the index definition uses [static mappings](/docs/search/index/define-field-mappings#std-label-static-dynamic-mappings) to index only the `title`, `genres`, and `_id` fields.  
```javascript
{
"mappings": {
"dynamic": false,
"fields": {
"title": {
"type": "string"
},
"genres": {
"type": "string"
},
"_id": {
"type": "objectId"
}
}
}
}
```  
**Example:**  
The following `find()` query finds the movie with the title "The Godfather" and stores the result inside `movie`. It specifies that the results should only contain the `title` and `genres` fields for the matching documents. Note that, by default, the `find
()` command always returns the `_id` field, the value for which might be different on your cluster.  
```javascript
movie = db.movies.find( { title: "The Godfather" }, { genres: 1, title: 1} ).toArray()
```  
**Output:**  
```javascript
[
{
_id: ObjectId("573a1396f29313caabce4a9a"),
genres: [ 'Crime', 'Drama' ],
title: 'The Godfather'
}
]
```  
The following query uses a [compound](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/compound#std-label-compound-ref) operator with the `moreLikeThis` operator to query the `title` and `genres` fields and the [equals](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/equals#std-label-equals-ref) to exclude the input document using the following clauses:  
- The `must` clause to query for movies similar to the movie stored in `movie`.  
- The `mustNot` clause to exclude the input document from results by its `_id` value. Note that the `_id` value used in the query matches the `_id` value in the results of the preceding `find()` query.  
The query limits the output to `5` results. The query uses a [`$project`](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/reference/operator/aggregation/project/#mongodb-pipeline-pipe.-project) stage to include the `_id`, `title`, `released`, and `genres` fields in the results.  
Before you run this query, replace the value of the `_id` field on line 13 with the value of the `_id` field in your query results.  
```json
