### Sort on Multi-Typed Arrays  
Consider the following queries on the [users collection](/docs/search/query/sort#std-label-fts-sort-index) given an additional document with a multi-typed array in field `c`:  
```json
db.users.insertOne({
"_id": 7,
"a": UUID("03e32aa9-1cbd-43b8-b9d6-18b171a03cc7"),
"b": "hello",
"c": [ false, null, 15 ]
})
```  
The following queries search field `b` for the string `hello` using the [text](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/text#std-label-text-ref) operator and sorts the results by field `c`.  
**Note:**  
Setting `noData: lowest` in your [sort syntax](/docs/search/query/sort#std-label-sort-syntax-ref) is the same as the default.  
#### Ascending Sort (Default)  
For an ascending sort, MongoDB Search uses the element with the lowest BSON (Binary Javascript Object Notation) type to represent the multi-typed array. By default, MongoDB Search considers null or missing values as the lowest BSON (Binary Javascript Object Notation) value. Therefore, MongoDB Search uses `null` to represent the multi-typed array for the document with `_id: 7` and returns this document at the top of the results along with other null and missing values.  
To learn more, see [Sort by Null and Missing Values](/docs/search/query/sort#std-label-fts-sort-null) and [Sort Arrays with Multiple Types.](/docs/search/query/sort#std-label-fts-sort-arrays-multiple-types)  
```json
db.users.aggregate([
{
"$search": {
"text": {
"path": "b",
"query": "hello"
},
"sort": {
"c": 1
}
}
},
{
"$project": {
"_id": 1,
"c": 1,
"score": { "$meta": "searchScore" }
}
}
])
```  
**Output:**  
```js
[
{ _id: 4, c: null, score: 0.025981096550822258 },
{ _id: 5, c: [], score: 0.025981096550822258 },
{ _id: 6, score: 0.025981096550822258 }
{ _id: 7, c: [ false, null, 15 ], score: 0.025981096550822258 },
