## Continue Learning

### Run Autocomplete and Partial Match Queries

You can use MongoDB Search to run autocomplete and partial queries that return results for partial words. This helps you retrieve results with increasing accuracy as more characters are entered in your application's search field. You must index the fields as the MongoDB Search [autocomplete](/docs/search/index/field-types/autocomplete-type#std-label-bson-data-types-autocomplete) type to use the [autocomplete](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/autocomplete#std-label-autocomplete-ref) operator.

To get started, see [How to Run Autocomplete and Partial Match MongoDB Search Queries.](/docs/search/tutorial/partial-match#std-label-partial-match-tutorial)

### Group Search Results with Facets

You can use MongoDB Search to run facet queries that group search results by string, date, or numeric values. You must create an index with a [facet definition](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/facet#std-label-fts-facet-definition) to use the [`facet` (MongoDB Search Operator)](/docs/search/query/operators-collectors/facet#std-label-fts-facet-ref) collector.

To get started, see [How to Use Facets with MongoDB Search.](/docs/search/tutorial/facet-tutorial#std-label-facet-tutorial)

### Use the MongoDB Search Playground

Use the [MongoDB Search Playground](https://search-playground.mongodb.com/). to try different MongoDB Search features by configuring search indexes and running queries without an Atlas account, cluster, or collection.
